      "id": "organ",
      "title": "Organ",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** instruments | **Difficulty:** 3/10 | **Time:** ~12 min"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "The pipe organ forces air through pipes of varying length — one of the largest and oldest mechanical instruments. Church and concert hall icon."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism",
      "title": "Mechanism",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Thousands of pipes; stops select which ranks sound. Manuals (keyboards) and pedalboard. Mechanical action (tracker) or electric/pneumatic."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sound",
      "title": "Sound",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Principals (flue pipes), flutes, reeds (trumpet stop), mixtures (multiple ranks). Enormous dynamic range — whisper to full tutti."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "composers",
      "title": "Composers",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Bach (*Toccata and Fugue in D minor*), Franck, Widor, Messiaen. Baroque organ temperament and registration differ from Romantic symphonic organ."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "related",
      "title": "Related",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Harmonium (reed organ, pressure bellows). Theatre organ. Hammond organ (electromagnetic tonewheels) in jazz and rock."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
